%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EBB980FC] ## BJT Discussion
%[text] There's a lot to unpack here. Not in any particular order: 
%[text] - $V\_{BE}$ rises (~linearly) with Vin \<~0.7V and then stays constant, not exceeding  \
max(vbe) %[output:087b804a]
%[text] - $V\_{CE}$ started at Vref, but when $V\_{BE}$ \> 0.5V, it fell off a steep cliff towards zero.
%[text] - $i\_B$ is around zero for Vin \<~ 0.6V (below the knee of the base-emitter diode) and rises linearly with Vin above the knee. When current rises linearly with voltage, the slope is 1/R, which we can estimate by eyeball: R = $\\Delta x / \\Delta y$ ~ 2$k\\Omega$ = the base resistor R4.  \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5D6D6026] ### An NPN switch is an Inverter or NOT gate
%[text] The collector voltage, $V\_{CE}$, is in a Boolean sense the negation of the input, i.e., this switch is a NOT gate, with truth table: 
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[130,134],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | <p align="center">$V\_{in}$</p> | <p align="center">$V\_{CE}$</p> |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | \<0.5V (logic 0) | \>4V (logic 1) |
%[text] | \>0.7V (logic 1) | \<0.2V (logic 0) |
%[text:table]
%[text] It's not "binary" in the sense that there <u>is</u> a continuous (analog) zone between 0.55V\<Vin\<0.7V where the collector has intermediate values. What makes a circuit like this approximately Boolean is the narrow transition zone, and the speed at which it can transition through that zone. All computer circuits are analog in this way, and what makes them "digital" is (drum roll) .... *a clock* that tells the system not to test or query the device's state until it has had time to settle into a (Boolean) final state. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_03AEC6F3] ### A transistor has power gain
%[text] Let's compute the power of the input (base) and output (collector) circuits, and display on both linear and semilog scales:
Prb = (ib/1e3).^2 * Rb; % watts
Prc = (vref-vce).^2 / Rc; % watts
subplot(1,2,1); %[output:23ac2c87]
plot(vin, [Prb Prc]*1000, 'd-') %[output:23ac2c87]
xlabel('vin / V'); ylabel('power / mW'); grid %[output:23ac2c87]
legend('base R power','collector R power','location','best') %[output:23ac2c87]
subplot(1,2,2);  %[output:23ac2c87]
semilogy(vin, [Prb Prc]*1000, 'd-') %[output:23ac2c87]
grid;  %[output:23ac2c87]
xlabel('vin / V'); ylabel('power / mW'); %[output:23ac2c87]
%[text] Figure 5. Power by base and collector resistors vs. input voltage. 
%[text] A small base current controls a much larger collector current. The ratio of these curves is the power gain: 
gain = Prc ./ Prb;
clf; % get out of subplot mode %[output:4465bbad]
semilogy(vin, gain, 'd-'); %[output:4465bbad]
grid; xlabel('vin/volts'); ylabel('power gain') %[output:4465bbad]
title('P_{collector} / P_{base}') %[output:4465bbad]
%[text]         Figure 6. BJT power gain.
%[text] Power gain drops as vin \>~.6V bceause once the transistor is turned fully on, the collector power is constant while the base power increases. Increasing base power often accelerates the transistor's switching speed.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8D503D48] ### Voltage divider loading error
%[text] My power supply puts out max(V) ~ 10.7V, which the 2k$\\Omega$+1k$\\Omega$ voltage divider should reduce to
10.7 / 3 % volts %[output:946bf30a]
%[text] But the x-axis (Vin) of all the graphs above ranges from ~0.5-2.9V. 2.9 $\\ne$ 3.6, so how can we explain that error? 
%[text] The answer is a common phenomenon with voltage dividers. The load -- in this case the base resistor and base-emitter -- are "pulling down" the voltage by drawing current. Here's how: 
%[text]    (a)![Fig_6A.png](text:image:65b0) (b) ![Fig_7b.png](text:image:7bb1)
%[text] Figure 7. (a) Fig. 1, and (b) equivalent circuit showing approximate loading of the voltage divider. 
%[text] The voltage divider equation we used only considered R2 and R3, but there are other current paths from the node between those two resistors. The lower right one in the schematic goes to A2 of the Arduino, but that's a high impedance (see References below), so no significant current flows. The upper path does draw current, and we already measured how much: max(ib) = 1.14mA (Fig.4, blue, is mA; Note we're ignoring the path to A1 for the same reason as A2). That seems like a little, but everything is relative. The total current through R2 (ignoring the parallel paths for the moment) would be 10.7V/(R2+R3) ~4mA -- so about 25% of R2's current could be hijacked through R4 instead of R3 -- which will affect the voltage divider. The base-emitter acts like a forward biased diode with Vf ~ constant 0.6V. Vbe $\\sim$0.6V $\\sim$ 0V when Q1 is on, so R2 is in series with approximately R3 || R4 = 667$\\Omega$, producing a maximum voltage ~ 10.7\*667/(667+2k)  = 
10.7*667/(667+2e3) %[output:30198b27]
%[text] That's less than we measured, and qualitatively, you should convince yourself that adding .6V to the bottom of R4 should push the voltage divider upwards, towards the voltage that we measured. With a little more effort you could solve this circuit more accurately.
%%
